# KNOWN GAP `t0872` — an inline-constructor receiver's temp is never registered
# for drop on the adapter path, and a plain struct-ctor field read leaks the
# same way. Heap-FORCED payload (`mk()`, never a literal).
#
# AXIS CELLS SAMPLED — the two still-leaking rows of the item's table:
#
#   combinator inline-ctor  `Some(mk(..)).map(f)`
#   struct-ctor field read  `Node(mk(..)).name`
#
# The named-receiver face (`t0772`) is CLOSED and is NOT here; it lives in
# `combinator_named_receiver_no_leak.gg`. The `.unwrap()` control
# (`Some(mk(..)).unwrap()`) is ASan-clean — inline-constructor temps ARE
# normally registered — so this is a registration gap at the adapter /
# field-read, not "ctors never drop".
#
# MEASURED at HEAD (`5d43cedd`), `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1`:
#   C:    Direct leak of 6 byte(s) in 1 object(s) × 2 (12 B / 2 allocations),
#         both from `gorget_str_cat` inside `mk`
#   LLVM: Direct leak of 12 byte(s) in 2 object(s), same site
# Stdout is `hello!` then `hello` (rc 0) — the leak is stdout-invisible, which
# is why this is an ASan fixture and not a `run_gg`.
#
# ⚠ Callable arguments are top-level functions rather than capturing closure
# literals (`t0526` would mask the 6 B signal with an 8 B env leak).
#
# INTENDED: prints `hello!` then `hello`, rc 0, ASan-clean under
# `detect_leaks=1`, on C and LLVM.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

String bang(String s):
    return mk(s, "!")

struct Node:
    String name

void main():
    print(Some(mk("hel", "lo")).map(bang).unwrap())
    String s = Node(mk("hel", "lo")).name
    print(s)
